[{:seon.task/name "a-check-run-never-loses-the-seed"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: `bin/check e2e loop` given a relative output path left the store's `:db` branch at 1,282 datoms instead of the seeded ~5,234, and every later `bin/check smoke` on it failed (`n-vars` 42, expected over 360); a fresh store passed. Correct: find what writes to `:db` (a check may only branch from it); `seon.e2e/loop-run` takes its directory as a required argument now ([dir])."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-check-run-leaves-the-seed-as-it-found-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-root-stops-on-a-signal-not-on-stdin"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's review of jolt/test-root, 2026-10-23: seon.main/root-serve loops (read-line) until its stdin closes, and bin/system stop ends a root by closing that pipe (bin/held's TERM closes the root's stdin): stdin as a lifecycle, AGENTS section 9's mistake, with one cause for two ends (a stop and a vanished holder). Correct: the root ends on a signal (its shutdown hooks already stop it on SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP: seon.main/root-serve's end!), bin/held sends TERM to the root's process (group) at its stop, the holder keeps no pipe to the root's stdin, and root-serve parks on the stop's promise instead of reading stdin (bin/mcp's relay and the owner's `bin/system stop` unchanged). Proof: bin/system stop on a root idle for 5 s ends it cleanly (ROOT STOPPED, exit 0) with the root's stdin /dev/null; a SIGKILLed holder ends it through the starter's fifo. Needs bin/held on main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/bin-root-stop-root-idle-5-s-stdin}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-hand-started-root-has-a-starter-or-says-it-stands"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the owner decides between requiring `bin/system start` to say it is standing and inheriting the session's starter, after the evidence below is gathered. Root's review of jolt/test-root, 2026-10-23: a root started without SEON_STARTER_FIFO does not die with its starter (the owner's standing root is meant to outlive the shell that started it; a root a worktree session starts by hand from its tool shell is not). bin/check and every test root always name a starter fifo (bin/held; seon.test-store/root-sh; the one-launcher check proves no test launches a root another way), so what remains is the hand start. Correct, a decision before code: either `bin/system start` requires its caller to say which it is (a standing root: --standing; any other names the starter, or is refused with the fifo it needs), or a root started from a session's tool shell inherits that session's starter. Evidence to gather first: how many `bin/system start` calls in session transcripts were never followed by a stop, and what each held (the 8 `seon lease` roots of 2026-10-23 were bin/check's, now covered). Proof: the chosen form refuses or ends an unnamed, unstanding root."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-runner-sees-its-childs-end"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (agent-view): a bin/test shard runner (test/test_runner.clj --shard, pid 44597) waited 1 h 55 m on a process test whose root process was gone: its worktree's target/seon was rebuilt under the running test. Nothing recorded that the child had ended; the shard never reported, and bin/test never ended. A wait for something that never came is a recorded fact, never a hang (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs). Correct: a process test's waits (the binary root it starts, its agents) end on the child's exit or socket EOF, and the test fails with that reason (which process, its exit status, the last it said), so the shard reports and bin/test ends. Proof: a process test whose root is killed mid-test fails at once naming the exit, and the shard's result says so."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-process-test-whose-root-dies-fails-with-the-exit}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-worktree-syncs-every-submodule-its-branch-pins"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (shrink session): `bin/worktree --sync NAME` moves only the submodules the worktree already has a checkout of; one main added after the worktree was made (reference-code/llama.cpp) is skipped without a word (its loop's `[[ -e $WT/$sub/.git ]] || continue`), so after merging main the worktree builds and tests without it and nothing says so. Correct: --sync does what setup does for a submodule it lacks (a worktree of the main tree's submodule repo at the branch's pin, and its nested copies), and says so; one it cannot make (the main tree has not checked it out, or lacks the pin) is named and the command exits non-zero, never silent. Proof: a worktree made before a submodule was added gets it from --sync, and one the main tree lacks makes --sync fail with its name."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/bin-worktree-sync-worktree-made-before-submodule-was}}
 {:seon.task/name "idle-waits-for-every-input-in-flight"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 (full-run-reds): the full run's idle was red under load (`:steps-at-idle {:render [5 6]}`, 16 bytes: one chunked `: settled` frame) and green alone. seon.check/idle now waits for the render proc's asks in flight (seon.render.proc/stats :pending, read by Flow's ping: the step that answers the last sends a settled mark). Not covered: a commit notice still on its way to the render proc (`:render-main` in the report, the render proc's :main-t behind main's t), whose step may send nothing the tab sees, so there is no mark to wait for. Correct: a barrier that answers after the render proc has taken main's commits up to a t, in order (as fork-server-caught-up does for the fork server), so the window opens on a render proc that holds main. If the red comes back, the report's :render-pending and :render-main name which case it was."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/bin-check-idle-under-load-answers-only-after}}

 {:seon.task/name "check-steps-are-tests-on-the-service"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Tests; a helper can port the steps one by one from this plan. What exists, 2026-11-11: each bin/check step is an item of the test service already (seon.prove/plan-items :kind :step, its digest seon.proofs/step-digests, its bound the step-end factor times its median ms), but its body is bin/check --steps on a seeded store copy (seon.fx run-item), a runner of its own (about 320 lines) beside the service. Plan: each mode of seon.check that drives a root becomes a deftest whose root runs on a branch of the one seeded store (one store, many branches); only window and resume (what a store holds after a kill and reopen) keep a store file of their own, and lmdb-kill and lmdb-lock their envs. A ported step is then an ordinary test item, keyed by its reach like any test, and leaves check-steps. Removes, once the last is ported: bin/check's runner and its per-step store copies, store-steps!, seon.proofs/check-steps. Order: the cheapest first (config, system, mcp, agent-checks). Proof per step: its deftest red under the mutation its step names, green on main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "test-inputs-sees-every-clock-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 (jolt/tests-cut): `bin/check test-inputs` (test/seon/check.clj test-inputs, docs/clocks.md) does not see a read of System/currentTimeMillis, one of its own hidden-inputs: a probe test file holding (defn probe-a [] (System/currentTimeMillis)) left :unnamed [] and TEST-INPUTS OK, and two real reads went unnamed (seon.results-test's age!, seon.e2e-test/await-quiet-ends-on-a-recorded-defect; both replaced on that branch). System/getenv reads in seon.config-test are seen, so the hole is in how that call's :form/refs are recorded or queried, not read yet. A clock read in a string sent to an agent or root (Thread/sleep in seon.agent-test/a-proof-run-ends-with-its-agent and seon.repl-test/a-reply-comes-after-its-commit) is no form ref at all. Correct: the check reads what the compiler records of every hidden input, so a probe of each one in hidden-inputs is red. Proof, red first: a check of test-inputs over a probe file reading each hidden input names each one."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-wait-on-a-quiet-root-that-cannot-come-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Every wait ends (AGENTS.md section 4); a wait on a predicate that can never become true ended only by bin/check's step bound (batch 24: the mcp check's await-records on an eval record :seon.eval/by \"outside\", which step 2 made a ref, hung 51.6 s twice). DONE (jolt/quietwait, 2026-11-11): await-records is deleted. An agent commits a request's writes before it replies (seon.repl/agent-step), so the mcp check and the two repl tests that waited on :commit events read the branch once from the store (seon.db/head) after the answer: no wait is left that a record that never comes could hang (docs/machine.md#builds-and-checks). A named seon.system/await-db already ends at the reconciler's quiet with :seon.system/quiet-before-true. LEFT: the UNNAMED await-db waits on facts another proc makes, which only a commit ends: the problems step's stall (test/seon/check.clj problems-check: during and after, the batch 32 overrun) and the vectors' due (four sites: scale, idle, pages, vector-check). The reconciler's quiet says nothing of the api's stall timer or the vectors proc. The honest end is each producer's own quiet: the vectors proc answering 'nothing due and nothing in flight' as the reconciler answers :quiet, and the stall check answering a :stall? it judged not a stall (the agent proc's verdict, sent back) so the step can end on 'checked past the bound, no stall' as a typed error, never a clock. FOUND 2026-11-11 (jolt/probwait): a wait's predicate on main runs IN MAIN'S WRITER at every commit (seon.system/await-db's watch; Datahike's writer resets the connection), so a predicate that rebuilds something over the whole db holds every commit behind it: the problems step's whole-report waits did (batches 32 and 38; fixed: one class and the reconciler's quiet). The same shape remains in test/seon/e2e.clj:259 (the reconciler's whole index per commit) and test/seon/check.clj's four seon.vector/due waits (all of main's texts per commit): each reads the one fact it waits on, or waits for its producer's quiet. Proof: the problems step with its stall check disabled ends red with that error instead of hanging; and a cost proof per wait that counts its predicate's whole-db reads over commits that do not satisfy it (as seon.check-test/the-problems-steps-waits-read-no-whole-report-per-commit)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}

 {:seon.task/name "health-reads-a-base-of-another-program-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 by the Tests session (jolt/tests-healthbase). Health builds the base's program with the base's own code in its own process, and the head reads the base's facts: a branch that changes the program's own attributes (:ns/, :var/, :form/ or the store's layout) is a typed HEALTH FAILED, since the head cannot read a base of another program schema. Before, the head analyzed the base with its own code, which collided across a namespace rename. Correct: the base's process answers health's base-side reads itself (what health asks of the base: its causes and classes), so no image reads another schema's store. Proof: a branch adding a :form/ attribute gets HEALTH OK."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/health-reads-a-base-of-another-program-schema}} {:seon.task/name "a-check-ends-its-timers-with-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: tests. Found 2026-11-12 (root): a bin/check whose prove ended red left a process of its own behind, pgid 30592, sleeping its 600 s checkpoint timer after the run was over; root ended it by hand. Cause (read in bin/check): the checkpoint timer (the subshell `sleep ${SEON_CHECK_EVIDENCE_AFTER_S:-600}`, W) and each unit's bound watcher (bounded) are ended only on the normal path after `wait`; the EXIT trap removes the starter fifo and the results dir and ends neither, so a run ended by TERM (its TERM trap exits 143) orphans them. Plan: the EXIT trap ends every timer the run started (W and the watchers bounded records), so whatever ends the run ends what it started; nothing else changes. Estimate: two kills at exit, ~0 work. Proof, red first: a bin/check selection sent TERM a second after it starts leaves no process whose parent was it (ps by parent before and after)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-check-ended-by-term-leaves-nothing-running}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-check-step-ended-by-wall-time-is-a-platform-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-14 (root's docs land check): bin/check's laws step failed \"STEP-OVERRAN, ran past its bound of 30000 ms (step-end-factor times its median; CPU 0.1 s, load 38.17)\" (bin/check:222): a check step's verdict decided by wall time under load, while its own work was 0.1 s of CPU. The same class runtime fixed for tests: a wall-time end is a platform problem (the run's evidence, filed through the error flow), never a red at the step's digest. Change: bin/check's overrun of a step (bin/check:194-222) answers no verdict and files a :platform problem with the step, its bound, its CPU and the load; nothing is cached at its digest. With the check-steps-as-tests work, a step's bound becomes its declared tick estimate like any test's (AGENTS 3, design 1's one rule), and wall time bounds nothing. Low priority beside Flow proving."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-step-past-its-wall-bound-is-no-verdict}}]
